{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** Each bundled skill's workflow must end by naming the skill for the next task, or the skill for each outcome. This is a per-site obligation. Each site's own review checks it, so I did not vet the sites.\n\n**Site query.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next/skills`. It selects exactly the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files, each by its front matter: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`.\n- A search for `name: habeas` across the repository finds no other skill file.\n- The files under `references/` have no front matter, so they are correctly not selected.\n- I found no missed places and no out-of-scope places.\n\n**Query snippets.** The match snippet is a document with front matter and a heading. The no_match snippet is the same document without front matter. Together they show the one condition the query has: recognizing a skill by its front matter.\n\n**Violation queries.** The requirement defines none.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_workflows_end_by_naming_the_skill_for_the_next_task]` is marked `{test}`. For each skill it takes the last numbered step of the Workflow section through `workflow_steps(name).pop()`. It then asserts with `assert_text` that the step contains the phrase naming the next skill.\n- `habeas-requirements` must name `habeas-evidence`.\n- `habeas-evidence` must name `habeas-requirements`, for findings that need code, tests or requirement text.\n- `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` must name `habeas-requirements`, for gap-list items.\n- `habeas-refactor` must name `habeas-evidence`, for reassessing stale evidence. It must also name `habeas-requirements`, for later behavior or text changes. This covers the case where different outcomes lead to different tasks.\n- `habeas` routes through its table, so the test requires \"Follow that skill\". It then asserts that the table names every other skill with `| {name} |`.\n\nI read the last steps in the skill files, and each contains the asserted wording. For example, the last step of `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` is step 9, and it contains \"Next: for gap-list items the user decides, follow the habeas-requirements skill\".\n\n**Why this suffices.** Skills with no further task for some outcome need no skill named for it, so the test has no obligation to check there. The assertions are content-specific and fail if a final step stops naming the skill. I found no adequacy gap.",
  "references": "cad77bef8fd9a6e827e81314c255639bcbcc74e3c865da250136997ac253fa61",
